WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:09.500
David: Marta, hey! Can you hear me okay? Your camera's frozen I think — no wait, there you are.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:16.000
Marta: Ha, yeah, sorry, hotel wifi. I'm still in Alicante, we drove down for the week. Everyone's at the beach except me apparently.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
David: Oh nice, I'm jealous. And thanks for taking this during your holidays, seriously. Twenty-five minutes max, I promise.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Marta: It's fine, honestly. If I don't take calls I just end up checking the queue anyway, so. You know how it is. What's this about again — you said it wasn't a support thing?

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:48.000
David: Right, no, nothing to do with support, and I'm not selling you anything either. I'm trying to understand how enforcement work actually happens day to day for people like you. Less about our tool, more about your Tuesday, basically. So maybe start there — what did your last normal workday look like?

00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Marta: Um, okay. So a normal day. I get in around 8:30, coffee, and the first thing is always the detection queue. That's non-negotiable because it piles up overnight, especially anything from Asia-Pacific marketplaces. Right now we're at something like 250, 300 new detections a day across all channels. I'd say the first two hours of every single day is just me and Jonas going through that queue.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.500
David: Two hours each, or two hours between the two of you?

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:31.000
Marta: Each. Yeah. And Jonas is technically only supposed to be on this half-time, he also does customs stuff. The detection side works great, honestly, I have no complaints there — it finds way more than we ever did manually. The problem is everything after.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.000
David: What do you mean, after? Walk me through what happens after you validate something.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Marta: So say I confirm a listing is counterfeit. Fine, one click, enforcement goes out. That part's fine. But then — okay, last month, real example. There's this seller network, we call them "the hydra" internally, which tells you everything. We took down forty-two of their listings on one marketplace in a single week. The following Tuesday, thirty-something new listings, same products, same photos even, under three new storefront names. And in the platform those are new sellers, new cases. All the history is gone.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.500
David: When that happened, what did you actually do?

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:34.000
Marta: What we always do. We keep our own spreadsheet — I'm not proud of this — a Google Sheet with, I think we're at 1,800 seller aliases now. Storefront names, the bank beneficiary names when we can get them from test buys, photos hashes Jonas does with some script of his. Every morning we cross-check new detections against the sheet manually. Because the platform sees storefronts, and we're fighting people, if that makes sense.

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
David: It does. How long has the sheet existed?

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:47.000
Marta: Since before me. My predecessor started it in 2022 I think? It's kind of a rite of passage, whoever's newest maintains the sheet. It's horrible, everyone hates it.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:57.000
David: You mentioned test buys. Is that a regular thing? Last time you did one, what triggered it?

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Marta: Yeah, so for the gray market cases — not counterfeits, actual product but unauthorized sellers — the marketplaces basically won't act without proof. So we do test buys. Last one was maybe three weeks ago, a seller in Poland undercutting our MAP by forty percent. We bought two units, photographed everything, serial numbers, the works. That whole dossier took Jonas most of two days. And then the marketplace rejected the complaint anyway, first round. Asked for a notarized authorization letter. It eventually went through on appeal, took nineteen days total.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.000
David: Nineteen days. Is that typical?

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Marta: For that marketplace? Yes, and that's a success story, ha. Counterfeits are fast, gray market is a swamp. We actually stopped enforcing gray market on two smaller platforms entirely — not worth the effort. Which drives sales insane, because those sellers show up in their channel reports.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:53.000
David: So when you decided to stop enforcing there — who made that call? Was that you?

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Marta: Me and my boss, our IP counsel. It wasn't formal, we just… stopped. There was a moment in the spring where I had, I want to say sixty open gray market cases, all waiting on evidence, and something had to give. Nobody above us ever noticed, by the way. Which maybe tells you something about how visible this work is.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:24.000
David: Hm. Actually — we're building some workflow automation around exactly this repeat-offender clustering, it would group those aliases automatically. Would that be useful?

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Marta: I mean, sure, sounds great. If it works. You know we've heard about a linking feature for a while now, ha.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:44.000
David: Fair. Forget I said that — I'm more interested in the sixty-case pile-up. When it got that bad, did you look at other tools, more people, anything?

00:04:44.000 --> 00:05:12.000
Marta: We asked for headcount. Got refused — hiring freeze, whole legal department. My boss then asked whether we could pay the agency we use for China filings to also do marketplace evidence packs, and finance actually said yes to that, weirdly. So there's money, it's just not allowed to become a salary. The agency does maybe ten dossiers a month for us now, they charge I think 180 per dossier? Which adds up, but it's the only thing keeping the gray market queue moving at all.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.000
David: That's really useful. So roughly 1,800 a month going to an agency for evidence work.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:26.000
Marta: More some months. December was brutal, obviously. Q4 everything doubles, the queue, the fakes, the panic from sales. Ha. December I don't take holidays, ever.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:35.000
David: Okay. If I could sit next to you for one of those 8:30 queue sessions — literally watch you and Jonas work through it — would that be weird?

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:50.000
Marta: Not weird at all, we do it on a call anyway since Jonas is in the Hamburg office. Honestly you should see the alias sheet in action, it's tragicomic. I'm back on the 26th, any morning that week works. I'll send you the recurring invite, it's at 8:45 Berlin time.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:57.000
David: Perfect, please do. And would Jonas be okay with me asking him about his photo-hash script?

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:08.000
Marta: He'd be thrilled, nobody ever asks Jonas about his scripts. I'll intro you on email. He also built the thing that exports our enforcement stats for the monthly report, which is its own nightmare, but that's a different conversation.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:15.000
David: I might take you up on that different conversation too. Last thing — anything I should have asked and didn't?

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Marta: Hmm. Maybe just — everyone always asks us about detection accuracy, surveys and stuff, and detection is fine. Detection has been fine for two years. Nobody ever asks what happens in the four hours after detection. That's where our week goes. Anyway. It's a good tool, David, we'd be lost without it, genuinely — but yeah. The four hours after.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:47.000
David: That's exactly what I needed. Enjoy the beach, say hi to the family, and I'll see you on the 26th at 8:45.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:52.000
Marta: Deal. Bye David.
